     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Federica,<br>  This kind of issue received a lot of attention for simpler SEM models in the early days of SEM.  Here are some general strategies.<br><br>1. Try using previous research to choose better start values.<br>2. Try fitting the model from a variety of different start values (this was automated in Mx).<br>3. Try extending the number of iterations, or refitting the model using the non-converged estimates as start values.<br>4. Try fitting a simplified model to obtain reasonable start values for some of the parameters and then use those to fit the full model.<br>5. Try to intuit potential problems from unusual or out-of-bounds values among the non-converged parameter estimates.<br><br>  It may be that in fitting simplified submodels, you would also discover parameters such as yours that can be fixed to zero.  Negative variances are nonsensical, and thus always indicate a problem somewhere in the model.  Nothing can vary less than a constant.<br><br>Keith<br>------------------------<br>Keith A. Markus<br>John Jay College of Criminal Justice, CUNY<br><a href=\"http://jjcweb.jjay.cuny.edu/kmarkus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jjcweb.jjay.cuny.edu/kmarkus&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422299035000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2UyedefiT6t0x4xtLLc9VS\">http://jjcweb.jjay.cuny.edu/kmarkus</a><br>Frontiers of Test Validity Theory: Measurement, Causation and Meaning.<br><a href=\"http://www.routledge.com/books/details/9781841692203/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.routledge.com/books/details/9781841692203/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422299035000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0mZGs1GSv9wBStkC4ApaSg\">http://www.routledge.com/books/details/9781841692203/</a><br><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
